{% block titre %}What do we mean by « Gregorian »?{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Where does Gregorian chant come from? A misleading name, eastern roots, the Carolingian unification, the birth of neumes in the 9th century and square notation: the whole story, simply told.{% endblock %}

{% block contenu %}
<h1>What do we mean by «&nbsp;Gregorian&nbsp;»&nbsp;?</h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    Before reading the neumes, let us go back in time. Where does this chant come
    from&nbsp;? Why does it bear the name of a pope&nbsp;? And why did an alphabet have to
    be <em>invented</em> in order to write it down&nbsp;? A short history, without jargon.
</p>

<h2>1. What exactly is Gregorian chant&nbsp;?</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    Gregorian chant is <strong>plainchant</strong>&nbsp;: a <strong>single melodic
    line</strong>, sung <strong>in unison</strong> (everyone on the same note), <strong>with
    no instrumental accompaniment whatever</strong>, and in Latin. No harmony, no
    orchestra&nbsp;: nothing but voices and a sacred text. This plainness is not
    poverty&nbsp;; it is a choice, turned wholly towards prayer and towards making the words
    intelligible.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Definition — Plainchant</span>
    <p>
        <strong>Plainchant</strong> (from the Latin <span class="mot-latin">cantus
        planus</span>, «&nbsp;even chant&nbsp;») is the monodic liturgical chant of the
        Latin Church. «&nbsp;Gregorian&nbsp;» is the name of its most widespread form, the
        one that established itself in the Carolingian period.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>2. A misleading name&nbsp;: Pope Gregory</h2>
<p>
    <strong>Gregory I, known as «&nbsp;the Great&nbsp;»</strong>, was pope from 590 to 604.
    He is one of the great figures of late antiquity&nbsp;: a tireless reformer, the
    organiser of the Church of Rome, a considerable spiritual author (we owe him the
    <em>Pastoral Rule</em> and the <em>Dialogues</em>). Tradition also credits him with
    reorganising the Roman liturgy and its <span class="mot-latin">schola cantorum</span>,
    the pope's school of singers. From there to making him the <em>author</em> of all the
    chant that bears his name was but one step… which the Middle Ages cheerfully took.
</p>

<h3>The legend of the dove</h3>
<p>
    Around 873, nearly three centuries after his death, a <em>Life</em> of Gregory tells a
    scene that was to make its fortune&nbsp;: the pope receives the melodies directly from
    the <strong>Holy Spirit</strong>, in the shape of a <strong>dove</strong> perched by his
    ear, while he dictates them to a secretary. Medieval iconography seized on the story and
    repeated it endlessly&nbsp;: Gregory writing, the dove on his shoulder, and often a
    scribe spying on him from behind a curtain, astounded to see him break off… and then
    resume, inspired.
</p>

<figure class="facsimile">
    <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/gregoire-colombe.jpg') }}" alt="Gregory the Great inspired by the dove of the Holy Spirit">
    <figcaption>Gregory the Great receiving the inspiration of the Holy Spirit in the form of a dove while dictating to a scribe — the so-called "Gregorblatt" miniature, <em>Registrum Gregorii</em>, Trier, Stadtbibliothek, Hs. 171/1626, circa 983. Source: Wikimedia Commons, public domain.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>What history says</h3>
<p>
    It is a <strong>fine legend</strong> — but a legend. Gregory died in 604&nbsp;; yet the
    repertory as we know it only took shape in the 8<sup>th</sup>–9<sup>th</sup> centuries,
    from the meeting of <em>Roman</em> chant with <em>Frankish</em> usage (we speak of a
    «&nbsp;Romano-Frankish&nbsp;» synthesis). Gregory may well have set liturgical reforms
    in motion&nbsp;; he did not «&nbsp;compose&nbsp;» these melodies, and he never saw a
    neume — they would appear only two and a half centuries after him.
</p>

<h3>Why the name stuck</h3>
<p>
    So why «&nbsp;Gregorian&nbsp;»&nbsp;? Because the attribution was
    <strong>useful</strong>. When the Carolingians set out to impose Roman chant on the
    whole of Europe (see below), there was nothing like placing it under the patronage of a
    prestigious pope&nbsp;: that gave it the authority of Rome and the prestige of sanctity.
    The «&nbsp;Gregory&nbsp;» <em>label</em> served as a seal of authenticity. The name has
    stuck&nbsp;; the reality is more collective, later — and, let us say it, more
    interesting.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Key point</span>
    <p>
        Gregory the Great is the <strong>symbolic patron</strong> of Gregorian chant, not
        its author. Remembering that is to understand that Gregorian chant is a
        <strong>collective work</strong>, shaped by generations of singers, and not the
        miraculous dictation of one man.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>3. Before Charlemagne&nbsp;: eastern roots</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    Christian chant did not come out of nowhere. The very first Christians were, for the
    most part, <strong>Jews</strong> familiar with the chant of the
    <strong>synagogue</strong> (<em>Hebrew cantillation</em>)&nbsp;: for them, singing the
    word of God went without saying. Around the Mediterranean they were also steeped in
    other traditions — <strong>Byzantine</strong>, and later <strong>Arab-Muslim</strong>
    (<em>Quranic cantillation</em>) — all of which share the same reflex&nbsp;: the sacred
    word is <em>sung</em>, it is not simply read.
</p>

