<h3>The legacy of the synagogue&nbsp;: psalmody</h3>
<p>
    At the heart of Gregorian chant are the <strong>psalms</strong> — those 150 poems
    inherited from the Hebrew Bible, sung for more than two thousand years. Christians
    inherited from them the practice of <strong>psalmody</strong> (singing a psalm to a
    simple melodic formula), but also its two great ways of doing so&nbsp;:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
<strong>responsorial</strong>&nbsp;: a soloist sings the verses and the assembly answers
with a refrain&nbsp;;
</li>
    <li>
<strong>antiphonal</strong>&nbsp;: two choirs answer one another in alternation — a practice
the West (St Ambrose in Milan) did indeed import from the East.
</li>
</ul>
<p>
    More striking still&nbsp;: some Hebrew words passed unchanged into the mouths of Latin
    singers. When you hear <strong><span class="mot-latin">Alleluia</span></strong> (from
    the Hebrew <em>Hallelou-Yah</em>, «&nbsp;praise the Lord&nbsp;»), <strong>Amen</strong>,
    <strong>Hosanna</strong> or <strong>Sabaoth</strong>, you are hearing a direct thread
    linking the synagogue to the choir of the monks.
</p>

<h3>Byzantium and the Christian East</h3>
<p>
    The other great neighbour is the <strong>Byzantine world</strong> (the Greek-speaking
    Christian East). To it we probably owe one of the most structuring features of Gregorian
    chant&nbsp;: the organisation of the melodies into <strong>eight modes</strong>. The
    Byzantines had their system of eight «&nbsp;tones&nbsp;» (the <span
    class="mot-latin">oktoechos</span>)&nbsp;; the Carolingian theorists set up, in
    parallel, the eight modes of the West. A direct borrowing is hard to prove — but the
    kinship of spirit is plain.
</p>

