</div>

<p>
    From the East too comes a certain <strong>taste for ornament</strong>&nbsp;: those long
    vocalises on a single syllable (the <strong>melisma</strong>, see the gradual <a
    href="{{ path('paleo_en_viderunt') }}">Viderunt omnes</a>) call to mind the fervour of
    eastern chants. It is no accident that ensembles such as <strong>Organum</strong>
    (Marcel Pérès) embrace, in their performances, this colouring from the East.
</p>

<h3>One and the same gesture&nbsp;: the sacred word sung</h3>
<p>
    As you can tell&nbsp;: three different worlds, one and the same fundamental gesture —
    carrying a sacred text on a melody, to engrave it in the memory and lift it above
    ordinary speech. Gregorian chant is one branch of that great Mediterranean tree.
</p>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">A historian's caution</span>
    <p>
        Talking about «&nbsp;influences&nbsp;» calls for modesty&nbsp;: since everything was
        handed down <strong>orally</strong>, we have no recording of these ancient chants.
        We reason by <em>kinship</em> and by clues (words, structures, written testimony),
        not by evidence of sound. So keep «&nbsp;family likeness&nbsp;» rather than
        «&nbsp;copy&nbsp;».
    </p>
</div>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Your question — can the scripts be compared?</span>
    <p>
        Yes, and it is fascinating&nbsp;! Several traditions independently invented signs
        «&nbsp;above the text&nbsp;» very close, in spirit, to our neumes&nbsp;:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
the Hebrew <strong>te'amim</strong> (cantillation accents of the Torah)&nbsp;: little signs
placed around the words to guide the melody — exactly the same idea of a gestural
aide-mémoire&nbsp;;
</li>
        <li>
<strong>Byzantine notation</strong> («&nbsp;palaeo-Byzantine&nbsp;» neumes), likewise at
first indicating the gesture, before becoming more precise&nbsp;;
</li>
        <li>
the <strong>Arab-Muslim</strong> tradition, long essentially oral (handed down from master
to disciple).
</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        In other words&nbsp;: everywhere, people first <em>memorised</em>, then wrote down
        <em>reminders</em> — never, at the outset, a complete score.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>4. The Carolingian unification</h2>
<p>
    By the 8<sup>th</sup> century the Christian liturgy was everywhere in Europe… but in
    <strong>very different forms from one region to another</strong>&nbsp;: «&nbsp;Old
    Roman&nbsp;» chant, Gallican in Gaul, Ambrosian in Milan, Mozarabic in Spain, Beneventan
    in southern Italy. As everything was handed down <strong>orally</strong>, each region
    sang «&nbsp;its own way&nbsp;», and the melodies drifted.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Charlemagne</strong> (following his father Pepin) wanted to unify his empire —
    through chant as well. He imposed <strong>«&nbsp;Roman chant&nbsp;»</strong> as the
    standard. From the meeting of that Roman chant with Frankish usage was born the
    repertory we call <strong>Gregorian</strong> today. The unification was as political as
    it was spiritual.
</p>

<h2>5. 9<sup>th</sup> century&nbsp;: the invention of neumes (about 850)</h2>
<p>
    One large problem remained&nbsp;: how to make sure that everyone sang the <em>same</em>
    thing, when nothing was written down&nbsp;? The answer appeared around
    <strong>850</strong>&nbsp;: people began adding little <strong>signs above the
    text</strong>, the <strong>neumes</strong>.
</p>
<p>
    Careful, this is the crucial point&nbsp;: these first neumes <strong>note neither the
    exact pitches, nor the intervals, nor a measured rhythm</strong>. They indicate only
    <em>how each syllable is to be sung</em> — rising, falling, pressing, lightening — and
    certain vocal effects. They are <strong>aides-mémoire</strong>&nbsp;: they serve to
    <em>recover</em> a melody one already knows by heart, not to discover it.
</p>

<h3>A demonstration&nbsp;: «&nbsp;Frère Jacques&nbsp;» in neumes</h3>
<p>
    To get a real feel for this, let us play a game&nbsp;: here is the beginning of
