    <p>
        That is the <em>whole</em> logic of the first neumes&nbsp;: a singer who knows the
        melody finds his bearings in it instantly&nbsp;; a beginner is left helpless. What
        is still missing is the information about <strong>precise pitches</strong>. That is
        exactly what square notation was to bring.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>6. 11<sup>th</sup> century&nbsp;: square notation</h2>
<p>
    From the 11<sup>th</sup> century onwards a decisive step was taken&nbsp;: the signs were
    aligned on a <strong>staff</strong>, which gradually settled at <strong>four
    lines</strong>. From then on, the height of a sign on the staff indicated the pitch of
    the sound. The notation does not give an <em>absolute</em> pitch (the choir adapts it to
    its voices), but it notes precisely the <strong>intervals</strong> between the notes.
    Here is the same beginning of <em>Frère Jacques</em>, this time «&nbsp;set down&nbsp;»
    on a staff&nbsp;:
</p>

