<h2>7. Solesmes&nbsp;: the great restoration</h2>
<p>
    Let us leap forward in time. Over the centuries Gregorian chant had become
    <strong>disfigured</strong>&nbsp;: melodies shortened, weighed down,
    «&nbsp;modernised&nbsp;» editions full of mistakes. In the 19<sup>th</sup> century a
    small abbey in the Sarthe was to change everything and give the chant back its authentic
    face&nbsp;: <strong>Solesmes</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Dom Guéranger and the Benedictine revival</h3>
<p>
    In <strong>1833</strong>, <strong>Dom Prosper Guéranger</strong> refounded the abbey of
    Saint-Pierre de <strong>Solesmes</strong> and restored there the Benedictine life,
    almost extinct in France after the Revolution. Passionate about the liturgy, he wanted
    to be done with the corrupt versions then in use and to <strong>go back to the
    sources</strong>&nbsp;: to recover the melodies as the early manuscripts had preserved
    them. That is the starting point of a scholarly adventure that would last more than a
    century.
</p>

<h3>Scholarship at the service of the chant</h3>
<p>Two monks were to turn that intuition into a rigorous method&nbsp;:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Dom Joseph Pothier</strong> compares the manuscripts and reconstructs the
        melodies&nbsp;; his <em>Mélodies grégoriennes</em> (1880), then his <em>Liber
        gradualis</em> (1883), mark the return of «&nbsp;true&nbsp;» Gregorian chant.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Dom André Mocquereau</strong> launches in <strong>1889</strong> the
        <strong><em>Paléographie Musicale</em></strong>&nbsp;: a monumental collection of
        <strong>photographic facsimiles</strong> of manuscripts from all over the world. By
        publishing side by side dozens of witnesses to the same chant, it proved, evidence
        in hand, what the original version had been. It is the direct ancestor of the
        approach of the <a href="{{ path('paleo_en_triplex') }}"><em>Graduale Triplex</em></a>.
    </li>
</ul>

<figure class="facsimile">
    <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/solesmes.jpg') }}" alt="The abbey of Saint-Pierre de Solesmes">
    <figcaption>The abbey of Saint-Pierre de Solesmes, birthplace of the revival of Gregorian chant since Dom Guéranger. Source: Wikimedia Commons, photo by Cliatop, public domain.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>The Vatican edition and the motu proprio of Pius X (1903)</h3>
<p>
    The work of Solesmes received a resounding endorsement. In <strong>1903</strong>, Pope
    <strong>Pius X</strong>, in his motu proprio <em><span class="mot-latin">Tra le
    sollecitudini</span></em>, proclaimed Gregorian chant the <strong>model for all sacred
    music</strong>. Hard on its heels came the <strong>Vatican edition</strong> — the
    <em>Kyriale</em> (1905), the <em>Graduale</em> (1908) — prepared with the help of
    Solesmes&nbsp;: it is the official reference in <strong>square notation</strong>.
</p>

