<h2>8. Today's performing styles</h2>
<p>
    How are these melodies sung nowadays&nbsp;? There is no single answer, but several
    schools — and that is precisely what makes listening so absorbing.
</p>

<h3>The classic «&nbsp;Solesmes method&nbsp;»</h3>
<p>
    To make the chant teachable, Dom Mocquereau devised a system of <strong>rhythmic
    signs</strong> (little dots, episemas, «&nbsp;ictus&nbsp;») added to the square
    notation. The result is the style most people have in mind&nbsp;: a <strong>flowing,
    even and serene</strong> chant, with notes of equal length grouped in twos and threes,
    of great purity (one thinks too of the chant of the abbey of <strong>Silos</strong>, in
    Spain). This was long the way taught everywhere.
</p>

<h3>From method to semiology</h3>
<p>
    In the 20<sup>th</sup> century, still at Solesmes, <strong>Dom Eugène Cardine</strong>
    pushed the study further&nbsp;: he scrutinised the <strong>earliest neumes</strong> (St
    Gall, Laon) to read in them nuances of rhythm and expression that square notation had
    erased. This is <strong>Gregorian semiology</strong> — and it is exactly what gave birth
    to the <a href="{{ path('paleo_en_triplex') }}"><em>Graduale Triplex</em></a> in 1979. In
    other words, «&nbsp;Solesmes&nbsp;» is not frozen&nbsp;: the house that fixed the even
    version is also the one that reopened the question of nuance.
</p>

<h3>Historically informed approaches</h3>
<p>
    Other artists go further still in restoring the old details, with more
    <strong>ornate</strong> performances, sometimes tinged with the eastern influences
    mentioned above. Such is the case of ensembles like <strong>Organum</strong> (Marcel
    Pérès) or the <strong>Ensemble Anne-Marie Deschamps</strong> (<em>Venance
    Fortunat</em>). Between the serenity of Solesmes and the exuberance of Organum there is
    no «&nbsp;good&nbsp;» and «&nbsp;bad&nbsp;» chant&nbsp;: there are two ways of bringing
    the same signs back to life.
</p>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">Hear the difference for yourself</span>
    <p>
        Each chant page brings together <strong>several performances</strong> to
