<h2>9. Going further</h2>
<p>
    Want to dig deeper&nbsp;? Here are a few doors in, from the most accessible to the most
    scholarly. (Always check the conditions of reuse of images, recordings and scores before
    publishing them on your site.)
</p>

<h3>📖 Books and references</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
<strong>Daniel Saulnier, <em>Le chant grégorien</em></strong> (Solesmes) — a short, limpid
introduction, ideal after this blog.
</li>
    <li>
<strong>Eugène Cardine, <em>Gregorian Semiology</em></strong> (Solesmes, 1970) — the
founding work on reading the early neumes&nbsp;; more technical.
</li>
    <li>
<strong><em>Graduale Triplex</em></strong> (Solesmes, 1979) — the book at the heart of this
blog, to be kept in front of you&nbsp;; and the <em>Graduale Romanum</em> for the square
notation alone.
</li>
    <li>
<strong>Willi Apel, <em>Gregorian Chant</em></strong> — a classic survey of the history and
the forms.
</li>
</ul>

<h3>🖥️ Digitised manuscripts (open access)</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
<strong>e-codices</strong> (virtual library of the manuscripts of Switzerland)&nbsp;: it
holds the great witnesses of <strong>St Gall</strong> — the <em>Cantatorium</em> (ms 359),
the antiphonary of <strong>Hartker</strong> (ms 390/391) — and <strong>Einsiedeln
121</strong>.
</li>
    <li>
<strong>Gallica</strong> (BnF) and the portals of municipal libraries&nbsp;: for many
manuscripts, including the <strong>Laon</strong> tradition.
</li>
    <li>
<strong>GregoBase</strong> and the <strong>Cantus Database</strong>&nbsp;: scores and
catalogues of Gregorian chants online, useful for tracking down a particular piece.
</li>
</ul>

